module Lang.ProjectionCliff where

{- THE PROJECTION CLIFF: projecting one record N times inside one expression, N = 1 .. 7.

   `.slow`, not `.e`, because at N = 7 this module DOES NOT COMPILE at the shipped
   defaults, and `core/test` type-checks every `.e` it finds. See
   `core/examples/incomplete/README.md` for the convention. `bin/ermine` will still run
   it if you name it explicitly:

       ERMINE_JAVA_OPTS="-Xmx3000m -XX:ActiveProcessorCount=2" \
         bin/ermine core/examples/Lang/ProjectionCliff.slow

   WHAT THIS MEASURES. `t ! f` on a record whose row is a VARIABLE is a `Has r f`, which
   is `exists c. r <- (f, c)` -- one existential row partition. N projections of the same
   record in one expression are N existential partitions over ONE whole, and the solver
   resolves them against one another. Measured on this file, one module per N, one JVM
   each, at `-Dermine.rowSound` ON / `dequeuePolicy=smallcanon` / `solveBudget=20000`:

       N   draws     saturated set   minted vocabulary   module load
       1       0             0                 0             0.14 s
       2       3             5                 1             0.11 s
       3      30            19                ~4             0.13 s
       4     207            65               ~11             0.21 s
       5   1,230           211               ~26             0.68 s
       6   6,783           665               ~57             2.00 s
       7  35,910 -- THE SHIPPED 20,000 BUDGET FIRES AT 20,009   2.93 s, REJECTED

   REVISED 2026-09-07 (`tracker/loopmodel/S4-DESIGN.md`, S4A review): the round-1 draw
   column was a MODEL run at one id base and read a few per cent high; the numbers above
   are the COMPILER's own counter, `-Dermine.rowTrace.draws=true`, and N = 7 was measured
   with `-Dermine.solveBudget=60000`. Both of the first two columns are exact closed
   forms:

       draws         D(N) = (5^N - 3*3^N + 2*2^N) / 2      -- same-lhs pairs COMPARED
       saturated set        3^N - 2^N                      -- strictly nested pairs S < T

   The third column is `--cycle`'s `maxmint`, the size of the MINTED-VARIABLE VOCABULARY
   at a step (`Cycle.lean:407`), not a per-key count, and it is the one column that is
   only approximate (id-base dependent). The per-key census is
   `looptrace --replay … --mints`, and over the whole of `Lang/` its maximum is **1** --
   as it is for every group in the E series. (E5's reviewer, L-1; the same mislabel was
   E4's M-1 and E3's P-18.)

   The ratio tends to a steady **5x** per extra projection (10.0, 6.9, 5.94, 5.51, 5.29),
   and **seven crosses the shipped budget**:

       Row solver resource limit reached (this is NOT a type error): the row constraint
       solver drew 20009 fresh row variables at this signature, past the
       -Dermine.solveBudget=20000 limit, so it was stopped rather than left to run.
       Raise the limit with -Dermine.solveBudget=<n>, simplify the row constraints at
       this signature, or report it.

   WHY IT MATTERS. This is not an exotic shape. It is what you write to render a row:

       showRows hdr (t -> [t ! a, t ! b, t ! c, t ! d, t ! e, t ! f, t ! g]) rows

   -- a markdown or CSV line for a SEVEN-column table. `Lang/TextTables.e`'s
   `catalogueMarkdown` does exactly this with five columns and is one of the three most
   expensive solves in the `Lang/` group (1,230 draws -- the same number as
   `catalogueAscii` and `RunningState.postingMarkdown`, because D(5) does not care what
   else is in the expression). A sixth column costs 6,783 and a seventh does not compile.

   FOUND TWICE, INDEPENDENTLY. E4's reviewer isolated the same cliff at the same time from the
   presentation side, with bare `p ! f` projections rather than a list literal, and measured
   3 / 33 / 207 / 1,243 / 6,795 / budget against the 3 / 31 / 207 / 1,241 / 6,956 / budget
   this file first reported -- the same curve, and on the compiler's own counter BOTH are
   3 / 30 / 207 / 1,230 / 6,783 / budget, because D(N) does not depend on the spelling. `core/examples/Present/shouldfail/proj01_seven_reads.e` is the
   negative for it; this file is the CURVE, which is why it is `.slow` and not a second
   negative. `tracker/loopmodel/E4-REVIEW.md` also records the stronger remedy: five
   projections under ONE WRITTEN PARTITION,
   `forall r o. (r <- ((| p1, ..., p5 |), o)) => {..r} -> String`, cost ZERO draws.

   THE FIX, MEASURED. E5's reviewer isolated the remedy on the group's own helper shape:
   reordering `showRows`'s arguments so the row-fixing one is checked first changes
   NOTHING, and annotating the lambda's argument at the concrete row takes the same call
   to **zero draws**. `TextTables.e` and
   `RunningState.e` now ship both spellings side by side. So:

   THE FIX A USER HAS. Give the lambda's argument a CONCRETE row. With
   `(t : {skuCode, skuTitle, …})` written out, or with the rows arriving from a
   `relation` whose header is fixed, there is no row variable and no existential at all.
   The cliff is a property of INFERENCE over a row variable, exactly as
   `core/examples/incomplete/README.md` says of the star-join cliff: "same eight
   dimensions, and the type annotation is the only difference."
-}

import Prelude
import Syntax.List
import List as L

field q1, q2, q3, q4, q5, q6, q7 : String

p1 t = [t ! q1]_L
p2 t = [t ! q1, t ! q2]_L
p3 t = [t ! q1, t ! q2, t ! q3]_L
p4 t = [t ! q1, t ! q2, t ! q3, t ! q4]_L
p5 t = [t ! q1, t ! q2, t ! q3, t ! q4, t ! q5]_L
p6 t = [t ! q1, t ! q2, t ! q3, t ! q4, t ! q5, t ! q6]_L

-- THE ONE THAT DOES NOT COMPILE:
p7 t = [t ! q1, t ! q2, t ! q3, t ! q4, t ! q5, t ! q6, t ! q7]_L

-- and the same seven columns with the row written out, which costs nothing:
p7fixed : {q1, q2, q3, q4, q5, q6, q7} -> List String
p7fixed t = [t ! q1, t ! q2, t ! q3, t ! q4, t ! q5, t ! q6, t ! q7]_L
